.TH PJ 1 2026-11-27 pajama "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pj \- open a tmux session per project
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pj
.RI [ PATH | NAME | \- ]
.br
.B pj save
.RI [ NAME ]
.br
.B pj stop
.RI [ NAME ]
.br
.B pj
.BR \-\-version | \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pj
opens one tmux session per project directory, by path, folder name, fuzzy picker or
history.
Each project keeps its window and pane layout, and the programs you choose come back
when it reopens.
.PP
Inside tmux
.B pj
switches the client to the session, outside tmux it attaches.
A layout is saved when you detach from its session, and on
.B pj save
and
.BR "pj stop" .
.PP
The projects
.B pj
knows are the ones in its history and the sessions it is running.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI pj " PATH"
Open the project at directory
.IR PATH ,
creating its session from the saved layout if it is not running.
An argument is a path when it contains
.BR / ,
is
.B .\&
or
.BR .. ,
or starts with
.BR ~ .
.TP
.BI pj " NAME"
Open the project whose folder is named
.IR NAME .
If several projects match that name, the picker opens.
.IP
.I NAME
matches project folder names and running session names.
Only letters, digits,
.BR . ,
.B _
and
.B \-
count, so status marks that agent tools such as omp add to a session name are
ignored:
.B main\[u23F3]
matches
.BR main .
.IP
.B pj
also switches to tmux sessions it did not create, such as one started with
.BR "tmux new" .
When such a session has the same name as a project,
.BI pj " NAME"
opens the project.
.B pj
never saves the layout of these sessions, because they have no project folder, but
.B pj \-
goes back to one you left with
.BR pj .
.TP
.B pj
Pick a project or session with fzf, most recent first.
.TP
.B pj \-
Go back to the previous project or session.
.TP
.BR "pj save " [ \fINAME\fR ]
Save the layout of session
.IR NAME ,
the current one by default.
.I NAME
is a session name as
.B tmux ls
shows it.
.TP
.BR "pj stop " [ \fINAME\fR ]
Save the layout and close the session.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the package name and version.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage.
.SH PICKER
A table of your projects and running sessions, most recent first, with columns for the
name, the window count, the active window, the time since last use, and the path.
.B \[u25B8]
marks the session you are in,
.B \[u25CF]
a running project, and
.B \[u25C7]
a session
.B pj
did not create.
A stopped project is dimmed and shows the window count and age of its saved layout.
.PP
The preview shows the
.B wt list
table of the folder's worktrees when
.B wt
(worktrunk) is installed, then the last 15 commits.
.B alt\-p
hides the preview, and
.B ctrl\-u
and
.B ctrl\-d
scroll it.
.SH RESTORING RUNNING PROGRAMS
When
.B pj
saves a session, it looks at the program running in each pane, and decides whether to
start it again when the session comes back:
.IP \(bu 2
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/pj/programs
lists program names, one per line, with
.B #
for comments.
A listed program is started again with its full command line.
.IP \(bu 2
An executable
.IB $XDG_CONFIG_HOME /pj/resume/ NAME
gets the PID of program
.I NAME
and prints the command to run instead.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IB $XDG_STATE_HOME /pj/ PATH .tsv
The saved layout of the project at
.IR PATH ,
with each
.B /
written as
.BR % .
.TP
.IB $XDG_STATE_HOME /pj/recent
Project history, most recent first.
.TP
.IB $XDG_CONFIG_HOME /pj/default.tsv
The layout for a project with no saved layout.
Without it a new project gets one shell window.
.TP
.IB $XDG_CONFIG_HOME /pj/programs
Programs to rerun.
.TP
.IB $XDG_CONFIG_HOME /pj/resume/ NAME
Resume script for program
.IR NAME .
.PP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
defaults to
.IR ~/.local/state ,
and
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
to
.IR ~/.config .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The project, session or history entry was not found, fzf is missing, or tmux failed.
.TP
.B 2
Bad arguments, or the picker is needed without a terminal.
.TP
.B 130
The picker was cancelled.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1),
.BR fzf (1)
